      "title": "Foundations: From Cells to Systems",
      "minutes": 20,
      "content": "<div class=\"anat-callout\">Understanding animal anatomy and physiology is the cornerstone of effective veterinary care. Whether assisting in surgery, monitoring an animal&rsquo;s health, or communicating with veterinarians, knowing how animal bodies are structured and how they function helps the medical team make informed decisions in real-world situations.</div><div class=\"anat-grid anat-grid--four\"><div class=\"anat-card anat-card--numbered\"><span class=\"anat-card-number\">1</span><h4>Key Concepts</h4><p>Grasp the basic principles of animal anatomy and physiology.</p></div><div class=\"anat-card anat-card--numbered\"><span class=\"anat-card-number\">2</span><h4>Directional Terms</h4><p>Use anatomical language to describe locations and directions in the animal body.</p></div><div class=\"anat-card anat-card--numbered\"><span class=\"anat-card-number\">3</span><h4>Animal Classification</h4><p>Recognize how animals are grouped and why classification matters in practice.</p></div><div class=\"anat-card anat-card--numbered\"><span class=\"anat-card-number\">4</span><h4>Organ Systems</h4><p>Identify the major organ systems and their primary functions.</p></div></div><div class=\"anat-grid anat-grid--two\"><div class=\"anat-card\"><h4>Anatomy</h4><p>The study of the structure and form of animal bodies. It focuses on how organs, tissues, and body systems are arranged.</p></div><div class=\"anat-card\"><h4>Physiology</h4><p>The study of how body structures function and work together to keep animals alive and healthy.</p></div></div><details class=\"anat-reveal\"><summary><span class=\"anat-reveal-q\">Why does this matter in veterinary medicine?</span><span class=\"anat-reveal-cta\">Reveal Answer</span></summary><p class=\"anat-muted\">Think about how structure and function are connected, then reveal the answer.</p><p>Anatomy tells us where structures are and how they are organized. Physiology tells us how those structures work. Together, they help the medical team recognize normal findings, detect changes, communicate accurately, and support safe patient care.</p></details><h3 class=\"anat-section-title\">Key Terms in Anatomy and Physiology</h3><p class=\"anat-muted\">Tap each card to review a foundational term.</p><div class=\"anat-flip-grid anat-flip-grid--terms\" data-anat-flip><button type=\"button\" class=\"anat-flip-card\" aria-pressed=\"false\" aria-label=\"Flip Anatomy\"><span class=\"anat-flip-inner\"><span class=\"anat-flip-face anat-flip-front\"><strong>Anatomy</strong><small>Tap to flip</small></span><span class=\"anat-flip-face anat-flip-back\"><strong>Anatomy</strong><span>The study of the structure and form of the animal body.</span><small>Tap to return</small></span></span></button><button type=\"button\" class=\"anat-flip-card\" aria-pressed=\"false\" aria-label=\"Flip Physiology\"><span class=\"anat-flip-inner\"><span class=\"anat-flip-face anat-flip-front\"><strong>Physiology</strong><small>Tap to flip</small></span><span class=\"anat-flip-face anat-flip-back\"><strong>Physiology</strong><span>The study of how body structures function.</span><small>Tap to return</small></span></span></button><button type=\"button\" class=\"anat-flip-card\" aria-pressed=\"false\" aria-label=\"Flip Organ\"><span class=\"anat-flip-inner\"><span class=\"anat-flip-face anat-flip-front\"><strong>Organ</strong><small>Tap to flip</small></span><span class=\"anat-flip-face anat-flip-back\"><strong>Organ</strong><span>A structure made of multiple tissue types working together.</span><small>Tap to return</small></span></span></button><button type=\"button\" class=\"anat-flip-card\" aria-pressed=\"false\" aria-label=\"Flip Tissue\"><span class=\"anat-flip-inner\"><span class=\"anat-flip-face anat-flip-front\"><strong>Tissue</strong><small>Tap to flip</small></span><span class=\"anat-flip-face anat-flip-back\"><strong>Tissue</strong><span>A group of similar cells that perform a shared function.</span><small>Tap to return</small></span></span></button><button type=\"button\" class=\"anat-flip-card\" aria-pressed=\"false\" aria-label=\"Flip System\"><span class=\"anat-flip-inner\"><span class=\"anat-flip-face anat-flip-front\"><strong>System</strong><small>Tap to flip</small></span><span class=\"anat-flip-face anat-flip-back\"><strong>System</strong><span>A group of organs working together to perform major body functions.</span><small>Tap to return</small></span></span></button></div><div class=\"anat-section-head\"><p class=\"anat-eyebrow\">LEVELS OF ORGANIZATION</p><h3>From Cells to Systems: The Building Blocks of Animal Bodies</h3><p>Travel through the levels of biological organization and discover how tiny cellular structures combine to create tissues, organs, body systems, and ultimately the complete animal organism.</p></div><figure class=\"academy-figure anat-figure\"><img class=\"academy-image\" src=\"assets/media/anatomy-physiology/types-of-animal-tissue.jpg\" alt=\"Types of animal tissue including nervous, cartilage, adipose, epithelial, blood, bone, muscle, and connective tissue\" width=\"908\" height=\"612\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>Different tissues have specialized structures and functions.</figcaption></figure><ol class=\"anat-timeline\"><li class=\"anat-timeline-item\"><span class=\"anat-timeline-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1</span><div class=\"anat-timeline-body\"><h4>Cell</h4><p>Cells are the smallest living units of the body. Each structure in an animal begins at the cellular level, from muscle cells that create movement to nerve cells that transmit signals.</p><p class=\"anat-timeline-note\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Veterinary professionals rely on understanding cellular function to recognize disease processes, handle specimens, and interpret laboratory findings.</p></div></li><li class=\"anat-timeline-item\"><span class=\"anat-timeline-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2</span><div class=\"anat-timeline-body\"><h4>Tissue</h4><p>Similar cells group together to form tissues that perform specialized jobs. Muscle tissue contracts, nervous tissue carries signals, and epithelial tissue protects surfaces.</p><p class=\"anat-timeline-note\"><strong>Clinical connection:</strong> Tissue health is evaluated in wound healing, surgical recovery, pathology, and diagnostic testing.</p></div></li><li class=\"anat-timeline-item\"><span class=\"anat-timeline-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3</span><div class=\"anat-timeline-body\"><h4>Organ</h4><p>Different tissues combine to create organs with specific functions. The heart pumps blood, the lungs exchange oxygen, and the liver processes nutrients and toxins.</p><p class=\"anat-timeline-note\"><strong>In practice:</strong> Understanding organ function helps veterinary teams monitor anesthesia, interpret diagnostics, and identify signs of disease.</p></div></li><li class=\"anat-timeline-item\"><span class=\"anat-timeline-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4</span><div class=\"anat-timeline-body\"><h4>Organ System</h4><p>Organs work together within body systems to maintain life. For example, the digestive system processes nutrients while the cardiovascular system transports oxygen and waste products.</p><p class=\"anat-timeline-note\"><strong>Whole-patient view:</strong> Changes in one system can affect many others, so veterinary assessment always considers the complete patient.</p></div></li></ol>",
      "quiz": [
        {
          "q": "Which term means a group of organs working together to perform major body functions?",
          "opts": [
            "System",
            "Tissue",
            "Cell",
            "Organ"
          ],
          "correct": 0,
          "exp": "A system is a group of organs that work together to perform major body functions."
        }
      ]
    },
