    {
      "id": 3,
      "icon": "▶",
      "title": "Organ Systems and Animal Classification",
      "minutes": 25,
      "content": "<figure class=\"academy-figure anat-figure\"><img class=\"academy-image\" src=\"assets/media/anatomy-physiology/canine-anatomy.jpg\" alt=\"Labeled internal organs of a dog\" width=\"1200\" height=\"900\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>Review the locations of the esophagus, trachea, lungs, heart, liver, stomach, spleen, small intestine, colon, rectum, and anus before exploring the organ systems.</figcaption></figure><div class=\"anat-section-head\"><p class=\"anat-eyebrow\">INTERACTIVE REVIEW</p><h3>Major Organ Systems Overview</h3><p>Click each card to reveal an important clinical connection or interesting fact related to veterinary patient care.</p></div><div class=\"anat-callout\">Organ systems work together continuously to maintain health. Recognizing how these systems function helps veterinary assistants and technicians monitor patients more effectively and communicate important findings.</div><div class=\"anat-flip-grid anat-flip-grid--organs\" data-anat-flip><button type=\"button\" class=\"anat-flip-card\" aria-pressed=\"false\" aria-label=\"Flip Skeletal System\"><span class=\"anat-flip-inner\"><span class=\"anat-flip-face anat-flip-front\"><span class=\"anat-flip-tag\">Flip</span><span class=\"anat-organ-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\">🦴</span><strong>Skeletal System</strong><small>Structure, support, and protection.</small></span><span class=\"anat-flip-face anat-flip-back\"><strong>Skeletal System</strong><span>The skeletal system protects vital organs, stores minerals, and works with muscles to enable movement. Lameness, fractures, and posture changes may signal skeletal problems.</span><small>Tap to return</small></span></span></button><button type=\"button\" class=\"anat-flip-card\" aria-pressed=\"false\" aria-label=\"Flip Muscular System\"><span class=\"anat-flip-inner\"><span class=\"anat-flip-face anat-flip-front\"><span class=\"anat-flip-tag\">Flip</span><span class=\"anat-organ-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\">💪</span><strong>Muscular System</strong><small>Movement, posture, and heat production.</small></span><span class=\"anat-flip-face anat-flip-back\"><strong>Muscular System</strong><span>Muscles do more than move the body. They also support breathing, maintain posture, stabilize joints, and help regulate body temperature.</span><small>Tap to return</small></span></span></button><button type=\"button\" class=\"anat-flip-card\" aria-pressed=\"false\" aria-label=\"Flip Cardiovascular System\"><span class=\"anat-flip-inner\"><span class=\"anat-flip-face anat-flip-front\"><span class=\"anat-flip-tag\">Flip</span><span class=\"anat-organ-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\">♥</span><strong>Cardiovascular System</strong><small>Blood circulation and oxygen delivery.</small></span><span class=\"anat-flip-face anat-flip-back\"><strong>Cardiovascular System</strong><span>This system circulates oxygen and nutrients while removing waste products. Pulse quality, gum color, and heart rate provide valuable information about patient status.</span><small>Tap to return</small></span></span></button><button type=\"button\" class=\"anat-flip-card\" aria-pressed=\"false\" aria-label=\"Flip Nervous System\"><span class=\"anat-flip-inner\"><span class=\"anat-flip-face anat-flip-front\"><span class=\"anat-flip-tag\">Flip</span><span class=\"anat-organ-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\">🧠</span><strong>Nervous System</strong><small>Control, coordination, and response.</small></span><span class=\"anat-flip-face anat-flip-back\"><strong>Nervous System</strong><span>The nervous system coordinates movement, reflexes, sensation, and behavior. Changes in mentation, gait, or pupil response may indicate neurologic concerns.</span><small>Tap to return</small></span></span></button><button type=\"button\" class=\"anat-flip-card\" aria-pressed=\"false\" aria-label=\"Flip Respiratory System\"><span class=\"anat-flip-inner\"><span class=\"anat-flip-face anat-flip-front\"><span class=\"anat-flip-tag\">Flip</span><span class=\"anat-organ-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\">🫁</span><strong>Respiratory System</strong><small>Breathing and gas exchange.</small></span><span class=\"anat-flip-face anat-flip-back\"><strong>Respiratory System</strong><span>The respiratory system supplies oxygen and removes carbon dioxide. Labored breathing, shallow respiration, or cyanosis require immediate attention.</span><small>Tap to return</small></span></span></button><button type=\"button\" class=\"anat-flip-card\" aria-pressed=\"false\" aria-label=\"Flip Digestive System\"><span class=\"anat-flip-inner\"><span class=\"anat-flip-face anat-flip-front\"><span class=\"anat-flip-tag\">Flip</span><span class=\"anat-organ-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\">🍽️</span><strong>Digestive System</strong><small>Food processing and nutrient absorption.</small></span><span class=\"anat-flip-face anat-flip-back\"><strong>Digestive System</strong><span>The digestive system breaks down food, absorbs nutrients, and eliminates waste. Appetite changes, vomiting, diarrhea, and stool quality are important observations.</span><small>Tap to return</small></span></span></button><button type=\"button\" class=\"anat-flip-card\" aria-pressed=\"false\" aria-label=\"Flip Urinary System\"><span class=\"anat-flip-inner\"><span class=\"anat-flip-face anat-flip-front\"><span class=\"anat-flip-tag\">Flip</span><span class=\"anat-organ-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\">💧</span><strong>Urinary System</strong><small>Waste filtration and fluid balance.</small></span><span class=\"anat-flip-face anat-flip-back\"><strong>Urinary System</strong><span>The urinary system filters blood and maintains hydration and electrolyte balance. Monitoring urine output is especially important in hospitalized patients.</span><small>Tap to return</small></span></span></button><button type=\"button\" class=\"anat-flip-card\" aria-pressed=\"false\" aria-label=\"Flip Endocrine System\"><span class=\"anat-flip-inner\"><span class=\"anat-flip-face anat-flip-front\"><span class=\"anat-flip-tag\">Flip</span><span class=\"anat-organ-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\">⚕</span><strong>Endocrine System</strong><small>Hormone regulation.</small></span><span class=\"anat-flip-face anat-flip-back\"><strong>Endocrine System</strong><span>The endocrine system regulates metabolism, growth, stress response, and reproduction through hormones released by glands throughout the body.</span><small>Tap to return</small></span></span></button></div><div class=\"anat-panel anat-panel--teamwork\"><h4>Teamwork for Patient Health</h4><p>Understanding organ systems helps veterinary assistants and technicians recognize abnormalities, communicate findings effectively, and support high-quality patient care.</p></div><h3 class=\"anat-section-title\">Animal Classification and Organization: Why It Matters</h3><div class=\"anat-hero-strip\"><p>Veterinary professionals encounter many species, each with unique anatomical and physiological traits. Classification helps organize those differences in a meaningful way.</p></div><p class=\"anat-lead\">Animal classification, or taxonomy, groups animals based on shared characteristics. In veterinary practice, understanding these groupings helps the team identify animals accurately, communicate clearly, and recognize species-specific care needs.</p><ol class=\"anat-rank-row\"><li>Kingdom</li><li>Phylum</li><li>Class</li><li>Order</li><li>Family</li><li>Genus</li><li>Species</li></ol><div class=\"anat-grid anat-grid--three\"><div class=\"anat-card\"><h4>Identify Accurately</h4><p>Classification supports correct species identification and helps veterinary teams understand similarities and differences between animals.</p></div><div class=\"anat-card\"><h4>Guide Patient Care</h4><p>Different species may have different anatomy, physiology, medication sensitivities, diets, housing needs, and handling considerations.</p></div><div class=\"anat-card\"><h4>Communicate Clearly</h4><p>Using shared classification language helps veterinary professionals communicate more precisely during exams, treatments, records, and referrals.</p></div></div><div class=\"anat-panel anat-panel--takeaway\"><h4>Key Takeaway</h4><p>Taxonomy is more than memorizing names. It helps veterinary assistants and technicians understand how animal groups are related and why those relationships matter in patient care.</p></div><figure class=\"academy-figure anat-figure\"><img class=\"academy-image\" src=\"assets/media/anatomy-physiology/taxonomy.png\" alt=\"Taxonomy classification of animals\" width=\"1280\" height=\"900\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>The hierarchy moves from Kingdom to Species.</figcaption></figure><div class=\"anat-callout\"><strong>Taxonomy sequence:</strong> Kingdom &rarr; Phylum &rarr; Class &rarr; Order &rarr; Family &rarr; Genus &rarr; Species.</div><div class=\"anat-sequence\" data-anat-sequence><h4>Put the hierarchy in order</h4><p class=\"anat-muted\">Click the terms from broadest to most specific.</p><div class=\"anat-seq-bank\" data-anat-seq-bank><button type=\"button\" class=\"anat-chip\" data-anat-rank=\"6\">Species</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"anat-chip\" data-anat-rank=\"2\">Class</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"anat-chip\" data-anat-rank=\"0\">Kingdom</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"anat-chip\" data-anat-rank=\"5\">Genus</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"anat-chip\" data-anat-rank=\"3\">Order</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"anat-chip\" data-anat-rank=\"1\">Phylum</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"anat-chip\" data-anat-rank=\"4\">Family</button></div><div class=\"anat-seq-zone\" data-anat-seq-zone aria-live=\"polite\" data-anat-empty=\"Your sequence will appear here.\"></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"anat-btn anat-btn--ghost\" data-anat-seq-reset>Reset</button><p class=\"anat-feedback\" data-anat-seq-feedback role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\" data-msg-wrong=\"That term is not next in the hierarchy. Reset and try again.\" data-msg-done=\"Correct! You ordered the complete taxonomic hierarchy.\" data-msg-partial=\"Correct so far &mdash; {n} remaining.\"></p></div><div class=\"anat-section-head\"><p class=\"anat-eyebrow\">TEST YOUR KNOWLEDGE</p><h3>Animal Classification</h3><p>Review each scientific classification and try to identify the common domestic animal before flipping the card.</p></div><div class=\"anat-callout\">These flashcards connect scientific taxonomy with familiar veterinary species and reinforce important anatomical and physiological traits.</div><div class=\"anat-flip-grid anat-flip-grid--animals\" data-anat-flip><button type=\"button\" class=\"anat-flip-card\" aria-pressed=\"false\" aria-label=\"Flip classification card for Dog\"><span class=\"anat-flip-inner\"><span class=\"anat-flip-face anat-flip-front\"><small>SCIENTIFIC CLASSIFICATION</small><strong>Class Mammalia,<br>Order Carnivora</strong><p>Has a simple stomach and non-retractable claws.</p><em>Tap to reveal</em></span><span class=\"anat-flip-face anat-flip-back\"><span class=\"anat-organ-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\">🐶</span><strong>Dog</strong><p>Domestic dogs are classified as carnivores even though they are commonly fed omnivorous diets in veterinary care settings.</p><em>Tap to return</em></span></span></button><button type=\"button\" class=\"anat-flip-card\" aria-pressed=\"false\" aria-label=\"Flip classification card for Cat\"><span class=\"anat-flip-inner\"><span class=\"anat-flip-face anat-flip-front\"><small>SCIENTIFIC CLASSIFICATION</small><strong>Class Mammalia,<br>Order Carnivora</strong><p>Known for retractable claws and a highly flexible spine.</p><em>Tap to reveal</em></span><span class=\"anat-flip-face anat-flip-back\"><span class=\"anat-organ-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\">🐱</span><strong>Cat</strong><p>Cats are obligate carnivores and require nutrients such as taurine that are essential for feline health.</p><em>Tap to return</em></span></span></button><button type=\"button\" class=\"anat-flip-card\" aria-pressed=\"false\" aria-label=\"Flip classification card for Cow\"><span class=\"anat-flip-inner\"><span class=\"anat-flip-face anat-flip-front\"><small>SCIENTIFIC CLASSIFICATION</small><strong>Class Mammalia,<br>Order Artiodactyla</strong><p>Has a four-chambered stomach for digesting tough plant material.</p><em>Tap to reveal</em></span><span class=\"anat-flip-face anat-flip-back\"><span class=\"anat-organ-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\">🐄</span><strong>Cow</strong><p>Cattle are ruminants and rely on microbial fermentation within the rumen to break down fibrous feeds.</p><em>Tap to return</em></span></span></button><button type=\"button\" class=\"anat-flip-card\" aria-pressed=\"false\" aria-label=\"Flip classification card for Chicken\"><span class=\"anat-flip-inner\"><span class=\"anat-flip-face anat-flip-front\"><small>SCIENTIFIC CLASSIFICATION</small><strong>Class Aves,<br>Order Galliformes</strong><p>Has feathers and lays eggs.</p><em>Tap to reveal</em></span><span class=\"anat-flip-face anat-flip-back\"><span class=\"anat-organ-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\">🐔</span><strong>Chicken</strong><p>Chickens are avian patients with lightweight skeletal adaptations and a unique respiratory system involving air sacs.</p><em>Tap to return</em></span></span></button></div><h3 class=\"anat-section-title\">Speaking the Language of Veterinary Anatomy</h3><div class=\"anat-panel anat-panel--summary\"><span class=\"anat-eyebrow\">Lesson Summary</span><h4>Accurate anatomical language supports safer patient care.</h4><p>Use directional terms, body planes, classification, and levels of organization to describe anatomy clearly and communicate findings consistently.</p></div>",
      "quiz": [
        {
          "q": "Which organ system is responsible for transporting blood throughout the body?",
          "opts": [
            "Digestive system",
            "Cardiovascular system",
            "Nervous system",
            "Integumentary system"
          ],
          "correct": 1,
          "exp": "The cardiovascular system circulates blood through the heart and blood vessels."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "cases": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "title": "Your First Patient: Bella",
      "species": "Canine",
      "patient": "Bella",
      "signalment": "5-year-old spayed female Labrador Retriever",
      "history": "Bella is a 5-year-old spayed female Labrador Retriever presented for vomiting since yesterday evening. She is quieter than normal but remains alert.",
      "stages": [
        "Palpation",
        "Auscultation",
        "Organ systems",
        "Directional terms",
        "Why it matters"
      ],
      "content": [
        "<p class=\"anat-muted\">Apply the concepts from Lesson 3 to assist with Bella&rsquo;s initial examination.</p><div class=\"anat-case-instruction\"><span class=\"anat-eyebrow\">Dr. Horner says:</span><h4>&ldquo;Please palpate the cranial abdomen.&rdquo;</h4><p class=\"anat-muted\">Select the correct region on Bella.</p></div><div class=\"anat-hotspots\" data-anat-hotspots=\"bella1\" data-anat-target=\"cranial-abdomen\" data-msg-correct=\"Correct! The cranial abdomen is immediately caudal to the thorax.\" data-msg-wrong=\"Try again. Cranial means toward the head.\"><div class=\"anat-dog\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Interactive dog diagram\"><span class=\"anat-dog-body\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"anat-dog-leg anat-dog-leg--1\"></span><span class=\"anat-dog-leg anat-dog-leg--2\"></span><span class=\"anat-dog-leg anat-dog-leg--3\"></span><span class=\"anat-dog-leg anat-dog-leg--4\"></span></span><span class=\"anat-dog-head\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><span class=\"anat-dog-tail\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><button type=\"button\" class=\"anat-hotspot anat-hotspot--cranial-abdomen\" data-anat-region=\"cranial-abdomen\"><span class=\"anat-sr\">Cranial abdomen</span></button><button type=\"button\" class=\"anat-hotspot anat-hotspot--caudal-abdomen\" data-anat-region=\"caudal-abdomen\"><span class=\"anat-sr\">Caudal abdomen</span></button><button type=\"button\" class=\"anat-hotspot anat-hotspot--thorax\" data-anat-region=\"thorax\"><span class=\"anat-sr\">Thorax and heart</span></button></div><p class=\"anat-feedback\" data-anat-hotspot-feedback role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\"></p></div>",
        "<div class=\"anat-case-instruction\"><span class=\"anat-eyebrow\">Dr. Horner says:</span><h4>&ldquo;Now auscultate the heart.&rdquo;</h4><p class=\"anat-muted\">Select the correct region.</p></div><div class=\"anat-hotspots\" data-anat-hotspots=\"bella2\" data-anat-target=\"thorax\" data-msg-correct=\"Correct! The heart is auscultated over the thorax.\" data-msg-wrong=\"Try again. The heart is located within the thoracic cavity.\"><div class=\"anat-dog\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Interactive dog diagram\"><span class=\"anat-dog-body\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"anat-dog-leg anat-dog-leg--1\"></span><span class=\"anat-dog-leg anat-dog-leg--2\"></span><span class=\"anat-dog-leg anat-dog-leg--3\"></span><span class=\"anat-dog-leg anat-dog-leg--4\"></span></span><span class=\"anat-dog-head\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><span class=\"anat-dog-tail\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><button type=\"button\" class=\"anat-hotspot anat-hotspot--cranial-abdomen\" data-anat-region=\"cranial-abdomen\"><span class=\"anat-sr\">Cranial abdomen</span></button><button type=\"button\" class=\"anat-hotspot anat-hotspot--caudal-abdomen\" data-anat-region=\"caudal-abdomen\"><span class=\"anat-sr\">Caudal abdomen</span></button><button type=\"button\" class=\"anat-hotspot anat-hotspot--thorax\" data-anat-region=\"thorax\"><span class=\"anat-sr\">Thorax and heart</span></button></div><p class=\"anat-feedback\" data-anat-hotspot-feedback role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\"></p></div>",
        "<div class=\"anat-case-instruction\"><span class=\"anat-eyebrow\">Knowledge Check</span><h4>Which organ system are you assessing when you auscultate the heart?</h4></div><div id=\"bellaDecision3\"></div>",
        "<div class=\"anat-case-instruction\"><span class=\"anat-eyebrow\">Clinical Application</span><h4>Bella&rsquo;s pain is located toward the head end of the abdomen. Which term best describes this location?</h4></div><div id=\"bellaDecision4\"></div>",
        "<div class=\"anat-case-instruction\"><span class=\"anat-eyebrow\">Final Question</span><h4>Why is it important to understand normal anatomy before assessing a patient?</h4></div><div id=\"bellaDecision5\"></div><div class=\"anat-panel anat-panel--why\"><h4>Why This Matters at Hannah</h4><p>Correct anatomical terminology helps doctors, nurses, and technicians communicate clearly, document findings accurately, and recognize subtle changes in a patient&rsquo;s condition.</p></div><div class=\"anat-case-complete\"><span class=\"anat-badge-medallion\" aria-hidden=\"true\">🏅</span><h4>Finish the case</h4><p>Complete this case to earn the <strong>Anatomy Explorer</strong> milestone for this lesson.</p><button class=\"primary\" id=\"completeL2Case1\" type=\"button\">Complete case</button></div>"
      ],
